Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:1:# LOB Crew Design Proposal v0
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:7:## What changed since the last proposal
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:10:- The seats split as BOMBANANA splits its monkeys: Site sees but cannot act, Rules knows but 
cannot act, Plan acts but cannot see. On the chart, Plan owns the planned lines, Rules owns the limits (permit 
boundaries and hold points), Site owns the actual lines.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:12:- Events slow the world instead of freezing it, so talk costs days. Four zones, the identical 
seed in Stage 2, rain rework that scales with open trench, and demobilization are decided or recommended below.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:15:- Stage 1 (push) has no huddle and the PM refuses preparation; Stage 2 (pull) adds a 30 second 
huddle and permits; Stage 3 is Florida, Las Vegas or Los Angeles. A conflict in Stage 1 stops the job and plays as an 
argument in VR.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:18:## The team and the three levers
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:26:| Rules | Paper | The limits: permit boundaries (open trench) and hold points | Knows but cannot 
act: diagnose, prescribe, issue permits, sign inspections | The spec book and the permit desk |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:37:## What each player can do
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:81:## The cooperation loop
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:90:4. Plan commits: lend the Standard excavator to clear the section and hold backfill for two days. 
The orders go out as radio calls. The event card states what happened and nothing more; in Stage 2 the trench box 
bought in the huddle simply makes the slump a smaller one, and the team finds out what it was spared only in the 
debrief.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:98:## The VR seat
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:107:  2. F2, pushing ahead: excavation crosses a zone boundary without a permit, and the city fines 
it. Natural in Stage 1, where every permit was issued; an authored deviation in Stage 2.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:113:## The field manual
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:138:## Paper kit
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:145:| Permit cards | Four numbered cards per stage, each with a 4-digit code and a check digit; all 
pre-signed by the PM in Stage 1 | The open-trench cap |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:146:| Inspection cards | One per zone, with the hold-point checklist and a card number | Clearing 
hold points |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:151:## World rules
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:155:- **Reporting.** The monitor is a planning surface fed by the daily report: its actual line 
steps forward once per simulated day with the day's feet, never the cause, the ground, or whether an order was obeyed. 
Site is live and carries the why. The plan is revised on the monitor; the reasons to revise it arrive by voice.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:157:- **No verdicts during play.** Nothing dings. The world shows consequences: a held crew bills 
and gets visibly angrier, an excavator at an unpermitted boundary idles, rework shows up as days, a fine shows up in 
the budget. Event cards state what happened and nothing else. The comparison of what would have happened, the 
plan-versus-actual overlay and the monitor's change log (every commit, with its day) appear only at the end of the 
stage. This follows R5 and the reference games, where a wrong call is discovered later, not announced.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:158:- **Permits.** A zone opens to excavation only when its permit code is entered on the monitor; 
the zone board in VR posts the permit number. In Stage 1 the PM has issued every permit. From Stage 2 Rules holds the 
cards, as many as the team chooses within the city limit, and a card returns when its zone is backfilled and signed. 
Excavation that reaches an unpermitted boundary idles and bills; excavation that crosses one is deviation F2 and draws 
a city fine.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:159:- **Hold points.** Backfill cannot close a zone until Rules signs the inspection card and Plan 
enters its number. A failed compaction test (B1) arrives as an inbox result.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:165:- **Win and lose.** The job is lost if funds go below zero or the deadline passes. Finishing is 
the win. There is no score; the stage's result shows finish day against the PM's promise, money against budget, 
crew-days idle, peak open trench, deviations caught and missed, fines, and each event with what softened it. Stage 2's 
result draws Stage 1's lines faintly underneath.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:168:## Stages
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:170:The demo is Stages 1 and 2. Stage 3 follows with the same build and new scenario files. Stage 2 
runs the identical seed (decided), so the overlay shows one world under two plans. Seats stay fixed through Stages 1 
and 2, so the same person feels push and then pull; seats rotate for Stage 3 and replays.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:172:**Stage 1, push, Texas, about 5 minutes.** No huddle. The PM's voice opens the stage: the plan 
is locked, crews start on Days 1, 2 and 5 with Standard machines, nothing is bought ahead ("not in the budget"), every 
permit is already issued. The clock runs. One deviation is authored (F1, the pipe crew keeps working after the hold); 
pushing ahead (F2) needs no authoring, since every permit was issued.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:174:| Day | Event | What the team does |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:181:**Stage 2, pull, Texas, identical seed, about 5 minutes.** A 30 second huddle, then the same 
world. Site rides the line and finds seepage between 6+00 and 9+00. Rules reads the Texas page (groundwater High, rain 
V.High) and sets the permit count. Plan moves backfill's start to Day 12 and buys a trench box at 6+00; Site confirms 
it was placed there (F3 if Plan named the wrong stake). The collapse still fires at 7+20 but on the protected branch; 
the rain on Day 18 meets less open trench; no crew collides. The result card overlays both runs: a later or equal 
finish, fewer idle crew-days, less rework, deviations caught.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:193:## Numbers
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:232:## Departures from LOB-Game's locked decisions
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:240:| SR18: no planning countdown | A 30 second mobilization countdown opens Stage 2 | Sets the 5 
minute pace; the run itself has no planning timer |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:244:| Decision 41: the approve and reject verb is out | Hold points are a three-seat handshake, and 
field deviations are inspection, not packet hunting | A different verb; flagged in case it reads as the same one |
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:248:## Build approach
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:258:- Order of work: a paper table test of Stage 1 (one afternoon, before any code), then a Stage 1 
vertical slice, then Stage 2, then Stage 3.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:260:## Decisions needed
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:264:- [x] Stage 2 runs the identical seed.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:270:- [x] Reporting: the monitor is a planning surface fed once a day by the report; the why arrives 
by voice from Site.
Docs\DESIGN_v0.md:271:- [x] No verdicts during play: facts at the event, consequences in the world, comparison and 
change log at the end of the stage.
